RAF WATCH

Interactive map of UK Royal Air Force activity using ADS-B data.

Hex codes are scraped from live-mobile-mode-s.eu. Flight traces come
from the public ClickHouse database behind adsb.exposed, which holds
unsampled airplanes.live and adsb.lol (ODbL) data from February 2023.
One SQL query per batch of up to 7 days (scripts/days.sql) selects
the listed hex codes and the UK military block (43c000-43cfff),
removes duplicate points and thins each trace. The source table is
not partitioned by date, so every query scans it; batching keeps the
load on the shared service low. scripts/fetch.py writes the result
as a compact binary file per day (RAF1 format), which the browser
parses. The frontend is static HTML/CSS and ES modules, with
MapLibre GL and the Inter font vendored in web/vendor and a keyless
OpenFreeMap basemap.

Setup

  uv run scripts/scrape_hex.py             Refresh hex codes
  uv run scripts/fetch.py                  Fetch missing or empty recent days
  uv run scripts/fetch.py 2025-01-01 2025-12-31
                                           Fetch (or refetch) a range
  uv run scripts/fetch_bases.py            Fetch RAF base locations from OSM
  python -m http.server -d web             Serve on localhost:8000
                                           (web/data links to ../data)

Deployment

  .github/workflows/pages.yml runs daily at 06:00 UTC and on every
  push. It downloads data.zip from the latest-data release, refreshes
  the hex list, fetches missing days, uploads data.zip again and
  deploys web/ to GitHub Pages. GitHub disables scheduled workflows
  after 60 days without repository activity, so the last step
  re-enables the workflow through the API to reset that timer.

  All data can be rebuilt from the source with fetch.py and a date
  range. A 7-day batch takes about 5 seconds.

Thinning

  Points are kept every 30 s in straight flight, every 5 s while the
  track changes by more than 1 degree per point, every 60 s on the
  ground, and at both ends of any gap longer than 60 s. Aircraft with
  fewer than 60 positions in a day are dropped as noise. Hours flown
  count airborne time between points less than 10 minutes apart.

Binary format: RAF1 (per-day .bin files)

  File header:
    [4 bytes]    magic "RAF1"
    [uint32 LE]  day epoch (UTC midnight as unix timestamp)
    [uint16 LE]  flight count

  Per flight:
    [uint24 LE]  ICAO hex code
    [uvarint]    trace point count

    Per trace point (delta-encoded from previous, initial = 0):
      [uvarint]   dt (seconds)
      [svarint]   dlat (units of 1e-5 degrees)
      [svarint]   dlon (units of 1e-5 degrees)
      [svarint]   dalt (units of 25 ft; -1 = ground)
      [svarint]   dspeed (knots)
      [uint8]     track (degrees * 256/360)

  Varints use LEB128 (unsigned) and ZigZag + LEB128 (signed).

Other files in data/

  manifest.json       {"2026-10-19": 312.4, ...} hours flown per day
  aircraft_meta.json  {"43c5df": {"reg", "type", "icao_type", "unit",
                      "heli"}, ...} for every aircraft seen
  aircraft.json       the scraped hex list

Data sources

  Hex codes    https://live-mobile-mode-s.eu/overviewall.php?DGCountry=UK
  Traces       https://adsb.exposed (airplanes.live, adsb.lol under ODbL)
  Basemap      https://openfreemap.org (OpenStreetMap)
  Bases        OpenStreetMap via Overpass API (military=airfield)
